<article class="env-lemma" id="05D3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05D3">Lemma <span data-tag="05D3">10.97.9</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $I \subset R$ be an ideal. Let $0 \to K \to P \to M \to 0$ be a short exact sequence of $R$-modules. If $M$ is flat over $R$ and $M/IM$ is a projective $R/I$-module, then the sequence of $I$-adic completions </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to K^\wedge \to P^\wedge \to M^\wedge \to 0  \]
</div>
  <p> is a split exact sequence. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      As $M$ is flat, each of the sequences 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to K/I^ nK \to P/I^ nP \to M/I^ nM \to 0  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is short exact, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00HL" data-tag="00HL">10.39.12</a> and the sequence $0 \to K^\wedge \to P^\wedge \to M^\wedge \to 0$ is a short exact sequence, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0315" data-tag="0315">10.96.1</a>. It suffices to show that we can find splittings $s_ n : M/I^ nM \to P/I^ nP$ such that $s_{n + 1} \bmod I^ n = s_ n$. We will construct these $s_ n$ by induction on $n$. Pick any splitting $s_1$, which exists as $M/IM$ is a projective $R/I$-module. Assume given $s_ n$ for some $n > 0$. Set $P_{n + 1} = \{ x \in P \mid x \bmod I^ nP \in \mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(s_ n)\} $. The map $\pi : P_{n + 1}/I^{n + 1}P_{n + 1} \to M/I^{n + 1}M$ is surjective (details omitted). As $M/I^{n + 1}M$ is projective as a $R/I^{n + 1}$-module by Lemma <a href="/tag/05CG" data-tag="05CG">10.77.7</a> we may choose a section $t : M/I^{n + 1}M \to P_{n + 1}/I^{n + 1}P_{n + 1}$ of $\pi $. Setting $s_{n + 1}$ equal to the composition of $t$ with the canonical map $P_{n + 1}/I^{n + 1}P_{n + 1} \to P/I^{n + 1}P$ works. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>